<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Distinguish a private assignment from an aggregate release, understand distinct-person support, and recognise refused identifiers and free text.
</div>

**You need:** the client packages and the Opal login from
[Getting started](book-00-start.html). This chapter uses `omop.questionnaire`,
the synthetic questionnaire study described there.

## Start this chapter

Log in first:

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
```

```{r chapter-setup, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.questionnaire", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r chapter-setup-local-2, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_connect("questionnaire")$conns
```

The connection is ready; `conns` identifies the server used by the analysis calls.

## Know which side holds each object

| On your computer | On each server |
|---|---|
| Recipe definitions, concept sets and parameters | Database records and credentials |
| Public table/column/vocabulary metadata | Pseudonymous person and episode rows |
| Output symbol names | Exact permitted dates, ages and derived columns |
| Disclosure-checked aggregates | Intermediate populations and joins |

A *symbol* is the name of a server object. Seeing the name does not give you its
contents. Pseudonymisation replaces person IDs for protected processing; it does
not make a patient-level table suitable for download.

```{r private}
private <- omop_recipe(variables = list(age = omop_age_at("2024-06-01"), sex = omop_sex()),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
recipe_execute(private, out = c(people = "D_private"), conns = conns)
```

The reply names `D_private`, the server table holding age and sex. Exact
completed age stays beside the source data; the table is available for approved
analysis calls in your DataSHIELD session.

<div class="book-flow" role="img" aria-label="Your recipe goes to the server; private rows stay there; checked summaries return to you.">
<div><strong>You</strong>Choose events, variables and outputs.</div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
<div><strong>Server</strong>Compute with private records and apply release checks.</div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
<div><strong>You see</strong>Symbol names, schemas and checked summaries.</div>
</div>

## Request an aggregate

```{r aggregate, table.digits = 2}
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_private$age", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.numNA("D_private$age", datasources = conns)
```

The mean completed age on 2024-06-01 is 42 years across 36 valid values;
12 people with year-only births have missing completed age. The roster includes
all people; a fixed-date age calculation does not establish survival to that date.
An approved
aggregate method checks its own disclosure rules. A successfully
assigned table does not guarantee every later summary or model will be releasable.

<div class="book-note">
**Note**

A private assignment is not a row download. Every later aggregate uses the disclosure rules of its own method.
</div>

## The final release rule

Each final patient component needs enough **distinct persons** for the server's
`nfilter.subset`. Many events from one person still contribute only one person to
this check. Our server sets `nfilter.subset = 5`, stricter than the
dsBase default of 3. Each data owner chooses the server policy.

| Rule | What it checks | Value on this server |
|---|---|---:|
| `nfilter.subset` | Distinct people in a final assigned patient table | 5 |
| `nfilter.tab` | Nonzero cells in a dsBase frequency table | 3 |

These checks apply at different steps. A table with enough people to assign can
still produce frequency cells too small to release.

Intermediate calculations may contain small populations privately. The gate is
at final publication; a joined result's private inputs do not need separate
published symbols. A standalone filter produces a final object and is gated.
Here we intentionally request a subset with no qualifying rows:

```{r small-refusal, warning=FALSE, error = TRUE}
ds.omop.filter("D_private", expr = omop_where(age > 200L),
  newobj = "D_too_small", conns = conns)
```

The refusal reports the policy boundary without providing the small private
count. The previous `D_private` object remains available. Empty and undersized
results share this protection; do not interpret the diagnostic as a patient count.

## Why identifiers and source text are refused

*Class 1* includes direct identifiers, source identifiers and free text that may
contain identifiers. Renaming a source field does not make it safe. Both output
values and predicates are checked, so a count cannot be used to probe a forbidden
text column. The following intentionally invalid definition demonstrates that check:

Fever question is **900002**, found in [chapter 4](book-04-vocabulary.html).

```{r blocked-text, error = TRUE}
blocked <- omop_recipe(variables = list(answer = omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900002L, value = "value_as_string"),
  reducer = omop_reduce("first_value"))),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
recipe_to_plan(blocked)
```

This definition is refused before execution. Use the reviewed coded answer
`value_as_concept_id` and translated categories when it answers the question,
as in [chapter 10](book-10-harmonise.html). Free text is not needed for that analysis.

## Why dates and ages can remain private columns

*Class 2* means a quasi-identifier: a value that may help identify someone when
combined with other facts. Exact dates, birth components and ages are permitted
in private server tables. Their
aggregates and any category labels still face the applicable release controls.

```{r date-allowed}
date_recipe <- omop_recipe(variables = list(visit_date = omop_date("first_visit")),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
recipe_execute(date_recipe, out = c(people = "D_private_date"), conns = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_private_date", datasources = conns)
```

The schema shows `person_id` and `visit_date`; it does not reveal the dates. Turning an exact date into a factor publishes
category labels as metadata, which needs its own support checks. A fine custom
date pattern can therefore be refused even when a private Date column is permitted.
Coarsen to the scientifically appropriate month/quarter/year and request an
approved aggregate; do not encode a disallowed field into another name or type.

## Protected keys cannot become analyst variables

```{r key-refusal, warning=FALSE, error = TRUE}
ds.omop.mutate("D_private", copied = person_id,
  newobj = "D_key_copy", conns = conns)
```

The server needs keys to align rows and joins; an analyst mutation cannot copy,
rewrite or filter them. Use the declared population and join operations instead.

## What the disclosure checks mean

*Class 3* covers ordinary analytic values, such as coded categories and numeric
measurements, rather than identifiers. It does not mean “safe to download”:
these values still remain private and their aggregates face release checks.
The analyses in this book use DataSHIELD disclosure controls and add no noise.
Some OHDSI analysis routes add noise fixed for each request, so repeating the
same request returns the same answer.

## Exercise 1: a nonempty subset can also be too small

Keep female participants whose completed age is 43 on the declared date.
The public questionnaire description places four people in this group. Predict
whether the server can assign that final subset.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r small-nonempty, warning=FALSE, error = TRUE}
ds.omop.filter("D_private", expr = omop_where(age == 43 & sex == "F"),
  newobj = "D_small_group", conns = conns)
```

The four-person group is below `nfilter.subset = 5`, so it is refused just as
the empty group was. The message does not disclose the small count; here the
count was known from the public teaching data. In a real study, retain a
scientifically justified larger population or report that the intended analysis
cannot be released.

</details>

## Exercise 2: did a refused copy create a table?

After the protected-key refusal, list the server objects. Is `D_key_copy` present?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r refused-object}
DSI::datashield.symbols(conns)
```

`D_private` and `D_private_date` still exist, but `D_key_copy`, `D_too_small`
and `D_small_group` are absent. A refused request did not create a usable table.
A schema or object name is metadata, not access to its patient rows.

</details>
## Recap

A recipe creates private objects and returns their names. Final patient outputs need distinct-person support; aggregates and category metadata have further checks. Class-1 fields and protected-key expressions are refused.

**Next:** [13. Visit dates, one idea at a time](book-13-visits.html).
